<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Maternus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius Maternus was a Roman traveller who, according to Ptolemy's Geography citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinus-of-tyre">Marinus of Tyre</a>, journeyed with the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> on an expedition south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> to the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agisymba">Agisymba</a>, said to have taken four months, in the late first century CE. His journey is one of the furthest documented Roman penetrations into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-maternus.jpg" alt="Julius Maternus" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cresthaven · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trans-Saharan_roman_expeditions-explorations.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
